{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1424 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ff48cc6487e3df5328651790f609ba86b17c4289935572d0bb32b40498a6d8da" :edb/db-before-t 1423 :edb/db-after-t 1424 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534736 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:30:36.648Z" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198084 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198084 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\:: \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n2. $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\:: \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n3. $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\:: \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198084 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)(x + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3$, and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4386/Source/Images/1312340fd7cc5c013bf4edfc5b131205.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is\n$x \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3$\nand can be expressed as $[MATH: x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).]$\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is\n$-3 \\leq x \\leq 3$\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3]$.\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1$. Therefore,\n$A = \\{\\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{3, 4, \\ldots \\}$.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2$. Therefore,\n$B = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}$.\nConsequently, $\\mid B \\mid = 7$, which is finite.\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q}$, the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2$. Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198084 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/3c2fc2f1eeb1d4e92f7933a408dafe7c3a216aa3d899f4025c71407140d47de1.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ \\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{ 3, 4, \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198085 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\: : \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198085 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\:: \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n2. $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\:: \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n3. $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\:: \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198085 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin (x\\pi) = 1\n$$\n\nfor $x\\in\\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxπ &= \\frac{π}{2} + 2mπ \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2} + 2m, m ∈ Z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ such that $\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2m \\in\\mathbb Z$. Therefore,\n$A = \\{\\}$.\nConsequently, $\\mid A \\mid = 0$, which is finite.\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m$, we have\n$B = \\left\\{\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\dfrac{9}{2},\\ldots\\right\\}$.\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subseteq C$, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198085 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\sin (x\\pi) = 1$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}+2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ such that $\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2m \\in\\mathbb Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A| = 0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\dfrac{9}{2},\\ldots\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subseteq C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198086 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $C =  \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198086 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x}) \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n2. $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x}) \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n3. $C = \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3}) \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198086 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{5x})=5{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{5x})=25{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(25{e}^{5x})=125{e}^{5x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6{e}^{-x})=6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nB = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2e^{x+3} \\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C| = 1,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198086 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n$f^{(0)}(x)|= e^{5x}; f^{(1)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{5x}) = 5e^{5x}; f^{(2)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(5e^{5x}) = 25e^{5x}; f^{(3)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(25e^{5x}) = 125e^{5x}; ⋮$\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n$A = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}$\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n$g^{(0)}(x)|= 6e^{-x}; g^{(1)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(6e^{-x}) =-6e^{-x}; g^{(2)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(-6e^{-x}) = 6e^{-x}; g^{(3)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(6e^{-x}) =-6e^{-x}; ⋮$\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n$B = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}$\nConsequently, $\\mid B \\mid = 2$, which is finite.\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n$h^{(0)}(x)|= 2e^{x + 3}; h^{(1)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(2e^{x + 3}) = 2e^{x + 3}; h^{(2)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(2e^{x + 3}) = 2e^{x + 3}; h^{(3)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(2e^{x + 3}) = 2e^{x + 3}; ⋮$\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n$C = \\{2e^{x+3} \\}$\nConsequently, $\\mid C \\mid = 1$, which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198196 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306609 13194139534736 true] [17592186198196 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306622 13194139534736 true] [17592186198196 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306629 13194139534736 true] [17592186198197 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306581 13194139534736 true] [17592186198197 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306588 13194139534736 true] [17592186198197 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306595 13194139534736 true] [17592186198198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306560 13194139534736 true] [17592186198198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306567 13194139534736 true] [17592186198198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306574 13194139534736 true] [17592186198198 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306602 13194139534736 true] [17592186198711 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534736 true] [17592186198711 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-5n+6>0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-5m+6<0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-5q+6<0}$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198711 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: n^{2} - 5n + 6 > 0}$\n2. $B = {m ∈ Z: m^{2} - 5m + 6 < 0}$\n3. $C = {q ∈ Q: q^{2} - 5q + 6 < 0}$" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198711 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x+6$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/0a42815f626652c332862a38a3812463a9407605329bd81df25f11274b26ce6f.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<2\\,\\text{or}\\,x>3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n2<x<3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(2,3).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-5n+6>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,0,1}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-5m+6<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-5q+6<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198713 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534736 true] [17592186198713 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi )=1}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2q\\pi )=1}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1}$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198713 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: \\sin (2nπ) = 1}$\n2. $B = {q ∈ Q: \\sin (2qπ) = 1}$\n3. $C = {x ∈ R: \\sin (2xπ) = 1}$" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198713 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+m\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{9}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198714 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534736 true] [17592186198714 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198714 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {f^{(n)}(x) = \\frac{d^{n}}{dx^{n}}(x^{2}): n ∈ N_{0}}$\n2. $B = {g^{(n)}(x) = \\frac{d^{n}}{dx^{n}}(\\ln x): n ∈ N_{0}}$\n3. $C = {h^{(n)}(x) = \\frac{d^{n}}{dx^{n}}(\\sqrt{x}): n ∈ N_{0}}$" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198714 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2 \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>3,$ we have ${f}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nA={{x}^{2},2x,2,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}})=\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\sqrt{x},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198719 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534736 true] [17592186198719 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡n=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡q=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." 13194139534736 true] [17592186198719 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: \\cos n = 0}$\n2. $B = {q ∈ Q: \\cos q = 0}$\n3. $C = {x ∈ R: \\cos x = 0}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." 13194139534736 false] [17592186198719 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there are no values of $m\\in Q$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},…}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198724 1017 #edb/ref 17592186195348 13194139534736 false] [17592186198724 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306616 13194139534736 true] [17592186198724 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534736 true] [17592186198724 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|2n|>5}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|2q|>5}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|2x|>5}$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186198724 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: \\mid 2n \\mid > 5}$\n2. $B = {q ∈ Q: \\mid 2q \\mid > 5}$\n3. $C = {x ∈ R: \\mid 2x \\mid > 5}$" 13194139534736 false] [17592186198724 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x|>5 \\\\ 2|x|>5 \\\\ |x|>\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|2n|>5$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {3,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|2q|>5$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306560 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306561 13194139534736 true] [17592186306560 1089 "q-181638" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306560 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534736 true] [17592186306560 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{3x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x+1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306560 1113 #uuid "1a8ae12a-aa30-5cf8-b983-e5939a4dd0ec" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306560 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-x})=-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-x})=-{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{-x},-{e}^{-x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x})=3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{3x})=9{e}^{3x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{3x})=27{e}^{3x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={{e}^{3x},3{e}^{3x},9{e}^{3x},27{e}^{3x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x+1} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x+1})={e}^{x+1} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x+1})={e}^{x+1} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x+1})={e}^{x+1} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{x+1}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=1,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only.\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306561 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306562 13194139534736 true] [17592186306561 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306563 13194139534736 true] [17592186306561 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306564 13194139534736 true] [17592186306561 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306565 13194139534736 true] [17592186306561 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306566 13194139534736 true] [17592186306561 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534736 true] [17592186306561 1041 "selection" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306561 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306566 13194139534736 true] [17592186306561 1110 #uuid "f21b1ab4-9164-524f-a8cb-2001bfc64e9b" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306562 1034 "$A$ and $C$ only" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306562 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306562 1111 #uuid "5fe319d7-7611-5c27-a109-e3c3394ab43b" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306563 1034 "None of the sets are infinite." 13194139534736 true] [17592186306563 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306563 1111 #uuid "bb720dd9-b1ce-593f-aae0-12a0d96a405b" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306564 1034 "$A$ and $B$ only" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306564 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306564 1111 #uuid "e865e59b-7275-5510-b3b4-dde31915850b" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306565 1034 "$C$ only" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306565 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306565 1111 #uuid "5144594e-7a9f-58eb-883e-d4c050150241" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306566 1034 "$B$ only" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306566 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306566 1111 #uuid "ab3e5bb6-82d6-50d3-9733-71053940aca5" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306567 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306568 13194139534736 true] [17592186306567 1089 "q-181643" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306567 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534736 true] [17592186306567 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?