true] [17592186306618 1034 "$A,B,$ and $C$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306618 1111 #uuid "c7c965ee-0e94-5a39-845f-e15323b94192" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306619 1034 "$B$ and $C$ only" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306619 1111 #uuid "ea7c8d98-bdee-5154-a979-7bdb7f6d8296" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306620 1034 "$A$ and $B$ only" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306620 1111 #uuid "86d4cdd7-8d7e-5aac-8f11-f456daa4ff28" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306621 1034 "$C$ only" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306621 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306621 1111 #uuid "7ebfff77-a585-5849-91e5-08a4ffb792ee" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306622 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306623 13194139534736 true] [17592186306622 1089 "q-181740" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306622 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534736 true] [17592186306622 1100 "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+1>0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+1<0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+1<0}$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306622 1113 #uuid "031a254f-645d-5e04-8e60-c9c3f41f5866" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306622 1145 "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/0699524ff20f871c9eeca93faa9934832b8e5816fd23b46862e8b01d48e7d86b.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-1<x<1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-1,1).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<-1\\,\\text{or}\\,x>1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+1>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+1<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+1<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306624 13194139534736 true] [17592186306623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306625 13194139534736 true] [17592186306623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306626 13194139534736 true] [17592186306623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306627 13194139534736 true] [17592186306623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306628 13194139534736 true] [17592186306623 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534736 true] [17592186306623 1041 "selection" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306623 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306626 13194139534736 true] [17592186306623 1110 #uuid "700b2c4c-c3f9-5c4f-8012-1b2f252111d6" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306624 1034 "$A$ and $C$ only" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534736 true] [17592186306624 1111 #uuid "f540d802-d458-5ac6-8630-46ef1a25e7bf" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306625 1034 "$A,B,$ and $C$" 13194139534736 true] [17592186306625 1103 #